The new ceramic case marks the first ceramic Apple Watch since the Series 5 Edition in 2019, but it is <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/10/ceramic-apple-watch-series-12-is-bigger/">slightly larger</a> than the other models and weighs up to 52.9 grams in its 46mm Night Blue configuration. Even the aluminum and titanium models are now 1mm wider and around 2 grams heavier than their predecessors.<br/>
<br/>
At the same time, the display appears to have <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/10/apple-watch-series-12-has-smaller-screens/">shrunk slightly</a>, even though its resolution is unchanged. Apple lists the 46mm Series 12 with 1,196 sq mm of display area, 2% less than the 1,220 sq mm of the Series 11, while the 42mm model drops 1.9% from 989 sq mm to 970 sq mm.<br/>
<br/>
A marginally smaller screen in a wider case means thicker borders, and Apple also appears to have pulled the case back slightly around a larger front glass, which could make them look even more prominent. The Series 10 went through a similar change in 2024, when its visible aluminum edge shrank compared to the Series 9 but its overall bezel, including the black border under the glass, actually grew. The shift is less dramatic this time around, since the Series 12 keeps the same basic case design. Here's how the two generations compare in size, weight, and display area:<br/>
