The most significant upgrade between the two devices is the new Health Sensing System. It uses a larger electrode surface for better skin contact during ECG readings, redesigned photodiodes laid out in a ring for more efficient light capture, and bigger, more power-efficient green LEDs. The result is that the Series 12 measures heart rate every five seconds throughout the day, 60 times more frequently than before, and samples HRV up to 24 times more often. Apple claims this gives the Series 12 the most accurate heart rate sensing of any wearable.<br/>
<br/>
That higher-frequency data powers several <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/apple-watch-series-12-and-ultra-4-health-and-fitness/">new health features</a> that are exclusive to the Series 12. The Readiness app weighs up your recent activity, training load, vitals, and sleep score to produce a single daily score out of 10, alongside a recommendation ranging from "Recover" to "Go For It." There is also a separate Recovery HRV metric, a daytime view in the Vitals app, and new watch face complications for real-time heart rate and step count. Apple has also rebuilt its pedometer model and says the Series 12 now offers the most accurate step tracking of any smartwatch.<br/>
<br/>
The Series 12 is the first model in two years to get a new chip, with the S11 replacing the S10 that powered both the Series 10 and Series 11, which itself was <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/09/09/no-new-apple-watch-chip-for-the-first-time/">almost identical</a> to the S9 chip from 2023. The new model also has <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/10/apple-watch-series-12-and-apple-watch-ultra-4-have-a-pleasant-surprise/">twice as much RAM</a>, with 4GB compared to 2GB in the Series 11.<br/>
<br/>
The S11 chip enables <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/audio-intelligence-features/">Audio Intelligence</a>, a new set of features exclusive to the Series 12 and Ultra 4. Live Rewind displays a transcript of the last 15 seconds of conversation with a double press of the Digital Crown, &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; Recap generates summaries of conversations, Sound Recognition alerts deaf or hard of hearing users to sounds like sirens and doorbells, and Shazam can automatically identify music playing nearby. According to Apple, audio is processed in a hardware-isolated Secure Exclave on the S11 chip and is never recorded.<br/>
<br/>
Everyday battery life is unchanged at up to 24 hours, or up to 38 hours in Low Power Mode. The improvements instead focus on workouts and charging. The Series 12 offers up to 10 hours of battery life during an outdoor workout, which Apple says is 25% more than the Series 11, and 15 minutes of charging now delivers up to 12 hours of use rather than eight.<br/>
<br/>
All other aspects, such as display resolution and brightness, 64GB of storage, 5G connectivity, and water and dust resistance all carry over from the Series 11 unchanged.<br/>
<br/>
<h3 id="is_it_worth_upgrading">Is It Worth Upgrading?</h3><br/>
For new buyers, the Series 12 is the obvious choice. It costs the same &#36;399 that the Series 11 did at launch, while adding a more capable health sensing system, double the RAM, and a considerable list of exclusive features. With a new chip and more memory, it is also likely to be better placed for future <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-intelligence/">Apple Intelligence</a> features. The Series 11 is the better value option only when it can be found heavily discounted, which is likely as third-party retailers clear their remaining stock.<br/>
<br/>
Last year, we concluded that virtually no users should upgrade from a Series 10 to a Series 11. This year, the picture is more nuanced. For most Series 11 owners, there is still no general-purpose reason to upgrade, since many key features are unchanged and &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI is coming to the Series 11 anyway. However, the Series 12 is worth considering if you rely on your Apple Watch for training and recovery insights, since continuous heart rate tracking, Recovery HRV, and Readiness are all exclusive to the new model. Deaf and hard of hearing users may find Sound Recognition a compelling reason to upgrade on its own, and some users will simply want the new ceramic finish. <br/>
<br/>
Series 11 owners who are happy to wait may prefer to hold out for next year's "Series 13," which is <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/30/apple-watch-redesign-coming-next-year/">rumored</a> to bring the first major redesign since the Series 10, including a new band attachment system.<br/>
<br/>
